{"experiments":[  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Replace edits lead to higher average target_delta than insert or delete edits across all molecular optimization tasks.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify how edit action_type (replace/insert/delete) affects target_delta and test for significant differences.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null target_delta.\n2. Compute mean and variance of target_delta grouped by action_type overall and within each task_id.\n3. Conduct one-way ANOVA on target_delta across the three action_type categories; follow up with pairwise Welch’s t-tests with Bonferroni correction.\n4. Fit an OLS regression: target_delta ~ C(action_type) + C(task_id) to estimate adjusted differences.\n5. Visualize results with boxplots of target_delta by action_type and faceted by task_id.\n6. Check ANOVA assumptions (homoscedasticity, normality of residuals) and report diagnostics.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of means, variances, and counts of target_delta by action_type (overall and per task).\n2. ANOVA summary table and pairwise t-test results.\n3. Regression coefficient estimates and p-values for action_type effects.\n4. Boxplots (and faceted boxplots) illustrating target_delta distributions by action_type."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Retaining the Murcko scaffold yields smaller magnitude changes in molecular weight (MW_delta) while producing larger QED_delta improvements in successful edits.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Assess whether murcko_scaffold_retained correlates with constrained MW changes and enhanced drug-likeness gains.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter to rows with non-null MW_delta and QED_delta.\n2. Group by murcko_scaffold_retained (True/False) and compute mean(|MW_delta|), mean(QED_delta), and variances.\n3. Perform Welch’s t-tests comparing |MW_delta| and QED_delta between retained vs non-retained groups.\n4. Fit two OLS models: one predicting |MW_delta| ~ murcko_scaffold_retained + C(task_id), another predicting QED_delta ~ murcko_scaffold_retained + |MW_delta| + C(task_id).\n5. Create a scatter plot of |MW_delta| vs QED_delta colored by murcko_scaffold_retained and overlay regression lines.\n6. Report model diagnostics and effect sizes.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Summary table of mean |MW_delta|, mean QED_delta, and sample sizes by scaffold retention.\n2. T-test results for differences in |MW_delta| and QED_delta.\n3. Regression summaries for both models.\n4. Scatter plot with regression overlays illustrating the relationship."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Longer natural‐language prompts (higher word counts) produce larger improvements in the target property (target_delta).",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether the verbosity of instructions correlates with the magnitude of achieved property changes.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and remove rows with null target_delta.\n2. Compute prompt_word_count and prompt_char_count for each row (tokenize by whitespace for words).\n3. Calculate Pearson and Spearman correlations between prompt_word_count (and prompt_char_count) and target_delta across the whole dataset and within each task_id.\n4. Fit an OLS regression: target_delta ~ prompt_word_count + prompt_char_count + C(task_id) + C(action_type).\n5. Plot scatter of prompt_word_count vs target_delta with a trendline, and annotate by task_id.\n6. Validate residual assumptions and report correlation coefficients and regression diagnostics.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Correlation coefficients with p-values for prompt length vs target_delta.\n2. Regression summary table showing coefficients for prompt length measures.\n3. Scatter plot(s) with trendlines and task_id annotations.\n4. Residual diagnostic plots."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Edits in the top decile of |MW_delta| produce smaller QED_delta improvements, indicating a trade-off between weight change and drug-likeness.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Determine whether extreme molecular weight changes negatively impact drug-likeness gains (QED_delta).",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter to non-null MW_delta and QED_delta.\n2. Compute the 90th percentile of |MW_delta| and flag rows with |MW_delta| ≥ that threshold as MW_extreme.\n3. Compute mean QED_delta and variance for MW_extreme vs non-extreme groups.\n4. Use Welch’s t-test to compare QED_delta between groups.\n5. Fit a regression: QED_delta ~ MW_extreme + SA_delta + C(task_id) to control for synthetic‐accessibility changes.\n6. Plot density curves of QED_delta for extreme vs non-extreme MW_delta and annotate effect sizes.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Group means, variances, and counts for QED_delta in extreme vs non-extreme MW_delta.\n2. T-test results for QED_delta difference.\n3. Regression coefficients with significance levels.\n4. Density plot comparing QED_delta distributions."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Edits that improve synthetic accessibility (SA_delta < 0) have a higher probability of being in the successful dataset than those that worsen it.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Quantify the association between SA_delta improvements and edit success status.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and train_neg.parquet, merge into one DataFrame with a binary column `success` (1 for train, 0 for train_neg).\n2. Drop rows with null SA_delta and compute SA_improved = SA_delta < 0.\n3. Create a contingency table of success vs SA_improved and perform a chi-square test of independence.\n4. Fit a logistic regression: success ~ SA_improved + C(task_id) + C(action_type).\n5. Evaluate model performance (ROC curve, AUC) and report odds ratios.\n6. Plot bar charts of success rates stratified by SA_improved.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Contingency table and chi-square test statistic with p-value.\n2. Logistic regression summary with odds ratios and confidence intervals.\n3. ROC curve and AUC.\n4. Bar plot of success rate by SA_improved status."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"The variability (variance) of target_delta is higher when the Murcko scaffold is not retained, and this effect differs across tasks.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Measure and compare target_delta variance by scaffold retention within each task.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter out null target_delta.\n2. For each task_id and murcko_scaffold_retained group, compute the variance of target_delta.\n3. For each task_id, perform Levene’s test to compare variances between retained vs non-retained groups.\n4. Compile a table of variances and p-values for all task_ids.\n5. Visualize the variances as a heatmap or clustered bar chart across tasks.\n6. Highlight tasks where variance differences are statistically significant after multiple hypothesis correction.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Table of target_delta variances by task_id and scaffold retention.\n2. Levene’s test results (statistic, p-value) per task_id with corrected significance flags.\n3. Heatmap or bar chart visualizing variance differences across tasks."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Changes in ring closures (difference in count of numeric ring‐closure markers in SMILES) correlate with synthetic accessibility changes (SA_delta): introducing ring edits increases synthetic difficulty.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Test whether edits that alter ring topology are associated with worse synthetic accessibility.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and drop rows with null SA_delta.\n2. For each row, count digits ('1'–'9') in input_smiles and output_smiles to get ring_count_in and ring_count_out.\n3. Compute ring_change = |ring_count_out − ring_count_in|.\n4. Calculate correlation (Spearman and Pearson) between ring_change and SA_delta.\n5. Fit a regression: SA_delta ~ ring_change + C(task_id) + C(action_type).\n6. Plot ring_change vs SA_delta with jitter and a trendline, coloring points by action_type.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Correlation coefficients and p-values for ring_change vs SA_delta.\n2. Regression summary showing ring_change effect.\n3. Scatter plot with trendline and coloring by action_type."  
    }  
  },  
  {  
    "hypothesis":"Prompts containing explicit numeric constraints (digits or comparison symbols '<', '>') lead to smaller MW_delta changes than prompts without numeric constraints.",  
    "experiment_plan":{  
      "objective":"Evaluate whether specifying numeric constraints in the instruction limits the magnitude of molecular weight edits.",  
      "steps":"1. Load train.parquet and filter to non-null MW_delta.\n2. Define prompt_numeric = True if prompt contains any digit or the characters '<' or '>'.\n3. Group by prompt_numeric and compute mean and variance of MW_delta.\n4. Conduct Welch’s t-test comparing MW_delta between numeric vs non-numeric prompts.\n5. Fit regression: MW_delta ~ prompt_numeric + C(task_id) to adjust for task effects.\n6. Create bar charts of mean MW_delta by prompt_numeric and overlay confidence intervals.",  
      "deliverables":"1. Mean, variance, and sample size of MW_delta for prompts with/without numeric constraints.\n2. T-test results (statistic, p-value).\n3. Regression summary for prompt_numeric effect.\n4. Bar chart of mean MW_delta with error bars."  
    }  
  }  
]}
